  "summary": "The Point Reyes Light reports that audits of Marin County Sheriff's Office Flock ALPR data show hundreds of thousands of unauthorized searches by out-of-state and federal agencies in late 2024, in apparent violation of California law and a 2022 ACLU/EFF settlement. The breach was not disclosed to plaintiffs, the public, or Marin's new inspector general, and comes as supervisors weigh a Peregrine real-time data dashboard and as Central Marin Police Authority and others cancel Flock contracts.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "three network audits\u2014from October, November, and December 2024\u2014revealed data sharing with out-of-state and federal agencies. In November 2024, out-of-state agencies conducted 254,131 searches of M.C.S.O. data, amounting to 48 percent of the total searches made that month.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "In August, the Central Marin Police Authority\u2014which services San Anselmo, Corte Madera, and Larkspur\u2014became one of the more than 150 law enforcement agencies to do so.",
      "paragraph_idx": 40
    },
    {
      "quote": "Johnson County conducted 22 searches of the M.C.S.O.'s Flock data in November 2024.",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    },
    {
      "quote": "Before Flock was implemented in Marin County, the M.C.S.O. took deliberate steps to disable nationwide data-access capabilities and ensure our system operated within strict privacy safeguards.",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    }
  ],
